  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nShortwave ridging is expected to maintain dry weather for most\nof the day as it slides east of the region. A shortwave trough\nwas analyzed across the Midwest this morning. Increasing\nmoisture ahead of this wave will continue to result in mid and\nhigh level clouds streaming across the region today. Shower and\nthunderstorm chances will begin to increase later this afternoon\nacross Ohio west of I-77 as a lead, initial shortwave approaches\nin SW flow ahead of the main trough. Highs are expected to be\nnear seasonable levels.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThe lead shortwave is expected to cross the region tonight, with\nsome support from a relatively weak low level jet. Elevated\ninstability could maintain a couple of thunderstorms as well.\nThe latest HREF probabilities for am inch of rain overnight are\nless than 10 percent across the region, though some signal for\nsome higher QPF is seen across NW PA. \n\nSome decrease in coverage of showers and thunderstorms is\nexpected Sunday morning after the passage of the initial\nshortwave, and as the low level jet weakens. The main shortwave\ntrough is progged to deepen into a stacked closed low through \nthe day Sunday, as it drifts across the Midwest and Ohio Valley \nregion. Most model guidance indicates a potential dry slot\nbetween the initial wave, and the main trough. If some breaks in\nthe cloud cover develop, this could result in increased diurnal\ninstability, with more cloud cover resulting in the opposite.\nCurrent ML CAPE projections generally range from 500-1500 j/kg,\ndepending on the amount of breaks in the clouds that occur.\n0-6km shear is expected to range from 30-40kt, with the higher\nvalues across Ohio. At the same time, a weak surface cold front\nis expected to drift across the Upper Ohio Valley region, which\ncould be the focus for more numerous showers and thunderstorms\nin the afternoon and evening. \n\nThe severe potential will be highly dependent on the amount of\nclearing and destabilization that occurs. The region continues\nunder a Marginal Risk, or level 1 out of 5, for severe storms\nSunday afternoon and evening. This still appears reasonable \nwith the amount of uncertainty in diurnal destabilization. The \nmain hazard is expected to be damaging wind. PWATS are progged \nto range from 1.6 to 1.8, which has trended downward slightly in\nthe most recent model guidance. Locally heavy rainfall is \npossible, especially where any training would occur.\n\nShowers and thunderstorms should continue into Sunday night as\nthe main shortwave trough drifts across the Upper Ohio Valley\nregion. The severe weather potential is expected to wane with\ndiminishing instability. \n\nEnsemble guidance indicates the upper low will persist across\nthe Upper Ohio Valley region as it weakens through midweek. The\nlow/trough should exit the region by late week, though broad\nupper troughing is expected to persist across the region through\nlate week. This pattern should result in mainly diurnally\ndriven, daily shower and thunderstorm chances.",
